  "notes": "Only one variant file exists for KZFL (\"KZFL_Solar Rooftop_Loan - modified.xlsx\"), compared line-by-line against the original (\"KZFL_Solar Rooftop_Loan.xlsx\"). Classified as RESTRUCTURE (not a pure rate change or simple reschedule) because three structural changes were found simultaneously, with the interest rate held constant at 6% throughout: (1) a second disbursement tranche of BDT 21,301,479 was added on 2022-12-28 (Drawdown table row on 'KZFL_Loan' sheet, cell C17:D18), on top of the original single first-tranche disbursement of BDT 120,708,380 on 2022-09-13; (2) the 12-month principal moratorium present in the original (first principal due 2023-09-30) was removed — the modified schedule begins amortizing principal from the second installment (2022-10-31); (3) the payment frequency during what was previously a quarterly interest-only stub (Sep'22/Dec'22/Mar'23/Jun'23 in the original) was changed to strictly monthly from the outset. Installment count rose from 112 (original) to 120 (modified), while the final maturity date stayed fixed at 2032-08-31 in both files, and the loan still fully amortizes to a closing balance of 0.\n\nBoth files' first row (2022-09-29, opening 120,708,380, interest 321,889.01, principal 0) are IDENTICAL, so the true point of divergence is the second installment (2022-10-31). I modeled effectiveDate as 2022-09-29 (the disbursement/drawdown date row, financial_close serial 44833) with loan_amount/disbursement = 120,708,380 (matching the ORIGINAL EX_DATA['KZFL'] first-tranche modeling convention, since EX_DATA['KZFL'].loan_amount field of 142,009,859 is only a sanction-amount label — the engine-modeled disbursements array in examples.ts sums to 120,708,380, all dated at serial 44817/first disbursement). Because the modified workbook's actual per-period principal/interest split cannot be cleanly regenerated from a single first-principles Annuity formula (the mid-life injection of the second tranche on 2022-12-28 creates an irregular balance jump from 120,003,819.91 to 140,556,979.89 between the Nov and Dec 2022 rows that a simple annuity engine would not reproduce without also modeling that tranche as a discrete disbursement event), I pinned the ENTIRE 120-row schedule via the override arrays (interest_payment_amounts / opening_balance_amounts / closing_balance_amounts), row-by-row, sourced directly from the 'Repayment Schedule' sheet of the modified workbook (columns: Repayment Date, Opening Balance, Interest Payment, Principal Payment, Closing Balance). repayment_dates are the corresponding Excel serials. This mirrors the override-array approach used for MCML/QPSL/SCBL/SKS/THERMAX in examples.ts.\n\nAn alternative, arguably cleaner modeling would treat the 2022-12-28 tranche as its own disbursements-array entry (i.e. disbursements: [[44833, 120708380], [44924, 21301479]]) with principal_type \"Annuity\" recomputed by the engine — but given the instruction to prioritize override-array accuracy over deriving a \"clean\" formula for irregular historical schedules, and because the workbook's own computed numbers are the ground truth to reproduce, the full-override approach was used instead. The stale assumption cells in 'KZFL_Loan' sheet rows 9-10 (Maturity date 120 months to 2031-09-13; Moratorium period 12 months to 2023-09-13) do NOT match the actual computed schedule below them and were disregarded as leftover/unedited label text from the original template — the actual computed 120-row table (which the 'Repayment Schedule' sheet also independently reproduces) was treated as authoritative. No overdue-amount roll-in, no waiver, and no regulatory reference (e.g. DFIM circular, BB approval) were mentioned anywhere in either workbook — this appears to be an internally modeled \"what-if\" restructure scenario rather than a formally executed one, so regulatoryReference was left null."
